## 6. Practice lab for your Ubuntu WSL

Run these commands in your terminal.
```bash

mkdir git-day9
cd git-day9

git init
git branch -M main

printf '# Calculator\n' > README.md

git status
git add README.md
git status

git commit -m "Initial calculator project"

echo "Addition feature" >> README.md
git diff

git add README.md
git commit -m "Add addition feature"

git log --oneline
```

If Git asks for your identity, configure it with your own details:
```arduino

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

You have now practiced creating a repository, staging changes, committing versions, comparing changes, and reviewing history. You do not need GitHub yet for this local lab.

## 7. Interview questions

1\. What problem does Git solve?

It tracks changes, maintains project history, and supports collaboration through repositories.

Hide answer

2\. What is the difference between git add and git commit?

git add stages selected changes. git commit records the staged changes in local repository history.

Hide answer

3\. What is a fork?

A fork is a separate copy of a repository, commonly created on a hosting platform to work independently or contribute changes.

Hide answer

4\. Can Git work without GitHub?

Yes. Git can initialize repositories, track changes, and create commits entirely on a local machine.

Hide answer

5\. What does git push do?

It sends local commits to a configured remote repository, subject to permissions and branch rules.

